<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Balliol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1249–<a href="/years/1314">1314</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as John I of Scotland, Toom Tabard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Balliol was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 1292 to 1296, chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> from among the claimants after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-maid-of-norway">Margaret, Maid of Norway</a>. A descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I and, through his mother Dervorguilla, of the lords of Galloway, he was humiliated by Edward's demands as overlord, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was deposed after the English invasion of 1296, which began the Wars of Scottish Independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-balliol.jpg" alt="John Balliol" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John,_King_of_Scotland_(seal).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Balliol's early life. He was born between 1248 and 1250 at an unknown place; Galloway, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> and Barnard Castle in County Durham are possibilities. His father, John, 5th Baron Balliol, founded Balliol College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. His mother Dervorguilla of Galloway was a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, Earl of Huntingdon, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a>. From her he inherited lands in Galloway; from his father, large estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Around 9 February 1281 he married Isabella de Warenne, daughter of the 6th Earl of Surrey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-maid-of-norway">Margaret, Maid of Norway</a>, in 1290, Balliol competed for the crown in the Great Cause. He was a great-great-great-grandson of David I through his mother, one generation further than his main rival Robert Bruce, 5th Lord of Annandale, but senior in genealogical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> administered the court, which decided for Balliol on 17 November 1292, and he was inaugurated at Scone on 30 November 1292.</p><p>Edward I, who had coerced recognition as Lord Paramount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, steadily undermined him, demanding homage, legal authority over the Scottish king in disputes brought by his subjects, and military support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He treated Scotland as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state and repeatedly humiliated the new king. A council of twelve appointed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> in July 1295 took over affairs, according to some English accounts, though Amanda Beam argues it was formed to help defend the realm. On 23 October 1295 Balliol concluded a treaty of mutual assistance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-alliance">Auld Alliance</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Edward invaded in retaliation, beginning the Wars of Scottish Independence. The English took Dunbar Castle on 27 April 1296, and Balliol abdicated at Stracathro on 10 July 1296. He was held in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> until 1299, when he was allowed to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and by about 1301 he lived on his family's estates at Hélicourt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>. He died there in late 1314.</p><p>His nickname Toom Tabard usually means empty coat, an ineffective king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> had no monarch until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> in 1306. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-balliol">Edward Balliol</a> later revived the claim to the throne with English support and had some temporary successes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Balliol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Noblemen headed by Edward I of England chose <b>John Balliol</b> as King of Scots over Robert de Brus, ending the succession dispute known as the Great Cause.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-17">17 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty between <b>John Balliol</b> of Scotland and Philip IV of France, signed in Paris, formed the Auld Alliance against England.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Châteaux de Hélicourt, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50,1.58&amp;year=1291" aria-label="Châteaux de Hélicourt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of John Balliol"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5087 5850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5087 5850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50,1.58&amp;year=1291">What was here in 1291? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
